// meteor-cfd case - SPEC-LIT S32's redesigned thermal-wall-function gate,
// rebuilt per SPEC-LIT S34 as a genuine 2-D PLANE channel rather than a
// 3-D duct: streamwise-cyclic in x, `empty` front/back in z (S34.1 -
// PatchKind::Empty, newly nameable in JSONC), hot walls top and bottom in
// y. There are no side walls left to confound anything - the whole point
// of this rebuild (see `channelPeriodicFluxLowRe.jsonc`'s own header for
// why the OLD 3-D duct version of this pair could not close the S32
// gate's resolved leg).
//
// mesh (a): `standard` wallTreatment, six wall-normal cells at y+ target
// 30-60 - unchanged from the duct version except the z axis, which is now
// `empty` (`cells: [8, 6, 1]`, was `[8, 6, 4]` with real `wallSide.*`
// walls there).
//
// >>> READ THIS FIRST: every number in this STATUS block and in the first
// >>> three UPDATE blocks below is SUPERSEDED. The S13.4.1/S32.5.5 UPDATE
// >>> block is the current measurement of this file AS SHIPPED; the S37 block
// >>> after it is a separate experiment run on scratchpad COPIES with one
// >>> token added (`PrtModel`, which defaults to `constant` and is absent
// >>> here), so it changes nothing in this file. The blocks in between are
// >>> kept, in order, as the record of how this gate got there.
// STATUS (SPEC-LIT S34, RUN and REPORTED - docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1 has
// the full table): converges to a bit-identical fixed point (`|U|` residual
// 1.4e-10, T unchanged in its last printed digit from iteration 5 000
// through 40 000). Measured y+ 56.8/57.7/58.5, T_w = 316.861 K,
// T_b = 292.92 K, U_b = 5.3696 m/s. For a plane channel D_h = 2H = 0.08 m
// (heated- and wetted-perimeter conventions coincide, unlike the old duct's
// two different numbers): Re = 28 638, Nu = 65.24 - Gnielinski -4.5%,
// Dittus-Boelter -11.5%, both inside their own band (**GATE CLOSES**).
// U_b/u_tau = 19.23 against the 15-17 a developed plane channel gives - no
// side-wall-drag caveat needed this time, since there is no side wall left.
//
// S32.1 diagnoses why the fixed-T version of this gate (`channelThermalWF`/
// `retired/channelThermalLowRe`/`channelPeriodicWF`/`retired/channelPeriodicLowRe`, four
// attempts, ratios 0.095/0.381/0.107) could never have converged on
// anything: with `Tw` imposed, the bulk temperature is a FREE output that
// settles wherever the wall conductance and the domain sink happen to
// balance, so the two meshes' fixed-T runs solved DIFFERENT problems (their
// own fourth attempt settled at ~50 K and ~3 K driving delta-T) and
// comparing their raw wall heat compared two products in which BOTH factors
// differed. S32.2's fix: impose the SAME q_w on both meshes, let each
// predict its OWN delta-T, and compare Nu = q_w D_h/(k(Tw-Tb)) against the
// Dittus-Boelter/Gnielinski correlations (S32.3) - the same shape every
// other validation in this project already has, per S32.3's own point.
//
// D_h for a plane channel (S32.2, S34): parallel plates separated by the
// full gap H give D_h = 2H, and the heated perimeter IS the wetted
// perimeter (both walls, nothing else) - the two conventions the old duct
// had to distinguish now coincide. H = 0.04 m (the y extent below), so
// D_h = 0.08 m - the SAME number the duct's own "heated perimeter"
// convention already used, kept on purpose so this rebuild is comparable
// to the duct numbers on record in docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1.
//
// q_w = 500 W/m^2 on each of the two hot walls (unchanged from the duct
// version - a property of the wall, not the cross-section). The
// compensating sink is `-2 q_w A_wall` (S32.2), with A_wall the area of
// ONE hot wall over the domain's streamwise length and its (nominal, unit)
// z depth: A_wall = 0.08 m * 0.04 m = 0.0032 m2 - the z depth is a single
// `empty` cell's width, carried only so the volume/area bookkeeping has a
// concrete number; it cancels in every per-unit-depth quantity this gate
// reports (T_b, U_b, Nu) the same way it always would in a true 2-D
// problem. Sink = -2*500*0.0032 = -3.2 W, IDENTICAL to
// `channelPeriodicFluxLowRe.jsonc`'s own, unchanged from the duct version
// since neither q_w nor A_wall changed.
//
// Momentum source unchanged (3.9 m/s^2, the duct version's own Blasius
// estimate for Re_Dh ~ 8000) - removing the duct's side-wall drag is
// EXPECTED to raise U_b (see the run report in docs/07-lowmach-solver.md
// S1.1), not something recalibrated away; the sanity checks in SPEC-LIT
// S34's own task (U_b below a laminar solve of the same mesh, U_b/u_tau in
// 15-17 for a genuinely developed plane channel) are what this rebuild is
// FOR.
//
// A fixed-FLUX wall over a domain with no other Dirichlet condition on T
// anywhere (cyclic streamwise pair, adiabatic-by-construction net energy
// balance, and now no side walls at all to be adiabatic on) leaves T's own
// absolute LEVEL undetermined by the discrete equation - only its
// gradients are - so the converged field sat at whatever level the initial
// condition put it at. This is the same null-space situation SPEC-LIT
// documents for pressure in a periodic domain (`src/simple.rs`'s own note
// on a `p` with no Dirichlet boundary anywhere), read for T rather than p -
// SPEC-LIT S35 names it precisely and S35.1's thermostat (below) is the fix,
// not a new numerical device on top of the old one.
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S35, thermostat): the `-heaterPower -3.2` compensation
// above is replaced by a `sources[]` thermostat (S35.1) targeting 293.15 K -
// this case's own `initial.T`/`TRef`, chosen so the already-recorded
// measurement below is REPRODUCED, not moved. Rerun: T_b = 293.283 K (was
// 292.92 K), T_w = 317.253 K (was 316.861 K), U_b unchanged at 5.3696 m/s -
// the small (~0.35 K) rise is the thermostat's own, exactly-predictable
// steady-state offset (T_mean settles at target + Q_wall*tau/(rho*cp*V),
// a P-controller's ordinary steady-state error under a persistent forcing,
// not a defect - see `src/sources.rs`'s `Thermostat` doc). Energy balance:
// thermostat power -3.2 W against 3.2 W of measured wall heat, difference
// 2.8e-7 W - round-off. Re = 28,638 (unchanged), Nu = 65.24 (unchanged to
// the last printed digit) - Gnielinski -4.5%, Dittus-Boelter -11.5%, BOTH
// still inside their own band: **GATE STILL CLOSES**. Two-initial-
// temperature regression (this thermostat's whole reason to exist, SPEC-LIT
// S35.2): rerunning the resolved twin (`channelPeriodicFluxLowRe.jsonc`)
// from T0 = 293.15 K and T0 = 400 K now converges to the IDENTICAL T_mean
// (293.574 K, every printed digit) from either start - see that file's own
// header and `docs/07-lowmach-solver.md` S1.1 for the full table.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S32.4/S32.5, WHICH FRICTION FACTOR - comments only; the
// case itself is byte-for-byte the one every number above was measured with):
// the Gnielinski -4.5% above is an ABSOLUTE-PREDICTION verdict, taken at
// Petukhov's SMOOTH-PIPE f = 0.02391. This leg also passes the REYNOLDS-
// ANALOGY verdict - Gnielinski at the f this leg itself realises, 0.02162
// (-9.6% BELOW the pipe correlation), gives Nu_Gn = 61.30 and +6.4%. S32.4
// requires every band statement to name which f it used; the two are
// different claims and the second is the weaker one. That f is INFERRED from
// the force balance on this file's own `bodyForce`, not measured at the wall
// - `ofgpu-lowmach` now measures the wall traction directly and prints both,
// but this case has NOT been rerun. See docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1.
// SUPERSEDED: it has since been rerun, and the measured f retired the
// Reynolds-analogy verdict above - see the S32.5.3/S35.3 block at the end of
// this header.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S32.5.3/S35.3 - THE GATE RERUN, and THIS FILE CHANGED):
// the thermostat above now carries `"weighting": "massFlux"`. Every number
// in the blocks above was measured with the UNIFORM sink and is kept as the
// record; the numbers below are what this file, as it now stands, produces.
//
// CONTROL FIRST: rerunning this case with the token set back to "uniform"
// reproduces every recorded number above to the last printed digit
// (T_b = 293.283 K, T_w = 317.253 K, U_b = 5.3696 m/s, Nu = 65.2386,
// thermostat power -3.2 W, y+ 56.80/57.69/58.50). That is what makes the
// pair below a controlled comparison and not two different states.
//
// THIS FILE AS SHIPPED (massFlux, 40 000 iterations):
//   T_b = 293.256 K | T_w = 317.567 K | dT = 24.3109 K | U_b = 5.36659 m/s
//   Re = 28,622 | Nu = 64.3168 (was 65.2386: -1.41%)
//   y+ 56.77/57.66/58.46 | thermostat power -3.20335 W against 3.2 W of wall
//   heat, a +0.105% gap (the uniform sink closed it to 2.8e-7 W)
//
// VERDICTS (S32.4 - every band statement names its f, and carries this leg's
// own energy-balance gap as an uncertainty on Nu):
//   * ABSOLUTE PREDICTION, Gnielinski at the Petukhov smooth-PIPE
//     f = 0.023911: Nu_Gn = 68.2975, measured is -5.8% - INSIDE +-10%.
//     Dittus-Boelter 73.7206, -12.8% - inside its own band. This leg still
//     CLOSES on both correlations, and its 0.105% energy gap is far narrower
//     than either margin.
//   * REYNOLDS ANALOGY, at the f this leg's WALL now MEASURES: f = 0.017247
//     (S32.5.1's wall-function form, rho u_tau^2 with u_tau = Cmu^1/4
//     sqrt(k_P)), Nu_Gn = 48.0673, measured is +33.8% - OUTSIDE. At the
//     VISCOUS form of the same measurement (0.019960) it is +14.4%, also
//     outside. The "+6.4%, inside the band" this header used to report was
//     taken at f = 0.02162, INFERRED from the body force - the inference was
//     8-25% high and the verdict it produced does not survive measurement.
//
// FORCE BALANCE (S32.5.2's correction - this crate's momentum equation
// carries no density, so the balance is taken in KINEMATIC units): body force
// (g.e_hat) V = 4.9920e-4 m4/s2 against a viscous wall sink of 4.98638e-4 -
// -0.113%, and +0.001% on the uniform control. THIS leg is where that
// identity was measured: `wall_shear`'s viscous form IS the discrete momentum
// sink, on a real turbulent flow. The rho u_tau^2 form misses the same
// balance by -13.6%, which is this wall function's own distance from local
// equilibrium, quantified for the first time.
//
// See docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1's last subsection for the full account,
// including the isothermal controls and a S13.4 defect found on the way
// (ofgpu-lowmach does not read numerics.div["div(phi,U)"] or
// numerics.relaxation.U at all).
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S13.4.1/S32.5.5 - THE GATE RERUN ON THE SETTINGS THIS FILE
// ACTUALLY ASKS FOR; comments only, the case itself is unchanged): every
// number in every block above was produced by a driver that read NONE of this
// file's `numerics` block. `ofgpu-lowmach` built its MomentumControls from
// `::default()`, whose convection entry is `bounded Gauss upwind` - so the
// momentum equation ran a first-order scheme WITH S3.1's bounded correction
// where this file asks for `Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)` with no `bounded`
// prefix at all. That is the S13.4 defect the S32.5.3 block above reports and
// leaves unfixed; it is fixed now, and both legs are rerun.
//
// CONTROL: setting `div(phi,U)` back to `bounded Gauss upwind` by hand and
// changing nothing else reproduces the record above to five significant
// figures (T_b = 293.256 K, T_w = 317.568 K, U_b = 5.36687 m/s, Nu = 64.3136,
// thermostat power -3.20335 W, drag balance -0.112%). The relaxation factors
// and solver tolerances this file names, which were also being ignored, are
// worth less than 1e-4 of the converged answer here.
//
// THIS FILE AS SHIPPED, at the settings it names (40 000 iterations):
//   T_b = 293.251 K | T_w = 317.483 K | dT = 24.2318 K | U_b = 5.3972 m/s
//   Re = 28,785 | Nu = 64.5257 (was 64.3168: +0.32%)
//   y+ 56.89/57.78/58.59 | thermostat power -3.2034 W against 3.2 W of wall
//   heat, a +0.106% gap | contErr 2.9e-8
//   f MEASURED = 0.017129 (rho u_tau^2) / 0.019760 (viscous)
//   kinematic force balance -0.005% (was -0.113%)
//
// VERDICTS (S32.4), all essentially unmoved on this leg:
//   * ABSOLUTE PREDICTION, Gnielinski at the Petukhov smooth-PIPE
//     f = 0.023878: Nu_Gn = 68.5979, measured is -5.9% - INSIDE +-10%.
//     Dittus-Boelter 74.0568, -12.9% - inside its own band. **THIS LEG STILL
//     CLOSES on both**, with a +0.106% energy-balance uncertainty far narrower
//     than either margin.
//   * REYNOLDS ANALOGY, at the f this leg's wall MEASURES: Nu_Gn = 47.9960 at
//     f = 0.017129 (+34.4%), 55.9339 at the viscous f = 0.019760 (+15.4%) -
//     OUTSIDE on both, unchanged.
//
// WHAT THE RERUN FOUND, which is not what it was expected to find: the
// first-order-vs-second-order half of the substitution is worth +0.22% of Nu
// on this leg and nothing to either conservation balance. The `bounded` half
// is worth the whole of the drag imbalance (-0.112% -> +0.002% on this leg,
// -3.787% -> -0.000% on the resolved twin). S3.1's bounded correction
// subtracts V_P (div u)_P on the argument that div u is an ERROR; in this
// low-Mach solver S25.1 makes it a prescribed CONSTRAINT, so on a MOMENTUM
// equation the correction removes real, converged momentum that never reaches
// a wall. SPEC-LIT S3.1 now forbids defaulting a momentum equation to it, and
// S32.5.5 has the four-run isolation.
//
// See docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1's last subsection for the full account.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S37 - THIS LEG AS THE CONTROL; comments only, THIS FILE IS
// UNCHANGED): S37 adds a selectable variable turbulent Prandtl number
// (Kays-Crawford), defaulting to `constant`, and this leg is the control for
// the experiment that measures it - see `channelPeriodicFluxLowRe.jsonc`'s
// own header and docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1's last subsection.
//
//   THIS MESH        constant        KaysCrawford     change
//   T_w              317.483 K       317.828 K
//   T_b              293.251 K       293.241 K
//   T_w - T_b         24.2318 K       24.5874 K       +1.47%
//   Nu                64.5257         63.5900         -1.45%
//   Prt in use         0.85            [0.8748, 0.8917], volume-mean 0.8817
//   energy balance    +0.106%         +0.110%
//   drag balance      -0.005%         -0.005%
//
// -1.45% against the resolved twin's -6.81%: a factor of 4.7, which is the
// asymmetry S37.3 predicted. This mesh's six wall-normal cells never reach
// the sublayer where Prt departs from 0.85, so there is almost nothing here
// for the model to correct.
//
// This leg is a NEAR-control, not an inert one, and its 1.45% decomposes
// exactly: +0.234 K of the 0.3556 K widening is (T_w - T_P), through the
// wall-face density read off the boundary value the (fr = 0) fixed-flux
// triple extrapolates through k_eff,wall; +0.121 K is (T_P - T_b), the
// interior profile responding to a core Prt 3.7% higher. The IMPOSED flux
// does not move - 500 W/m2 exactly in both runs, since `fixedFluxTemperature`
// writes ref_grad = q/k_eff,wall.
//
// Both verdicts are unchanged in substance: Gnielinski at the pipe f -5.9% ->
// -7.3% (inside +-10% either way), Dittus-Boelter -12.9% -> -14.1%, and the
// Reynolds-analogy miss +34.4% -> +34.1% - that one is a FRICTION finding
// (this mesh's rho u_tau^2 form sits 13.6% below the viscous form on the same
// faces) and S37 neither touches nor moves it.
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// UPDATE (SPEC-LIT S26.1 - THE ENERGY IMBALANCE IS CLOSED; comments only, THIS
// FILE IS UNCHANGED): S25.1 defines Q = q'''_c + div(k_eff grad T) - div(q_r)
// and this crate implemented Q as the S18 registry alone, leaving out the
// CONDUCTION term. The same Q builds (div u)_target, so the pressure equation
// was prescribing the wrong dilatation wherever the fluid conducts heat. On
// the resolved twin that was the whole of a +3.11% energy imbalance; on THIS
// leg it was +0.106%, which is why this leg was always the control.
//
// THIS FILE AS SHIPPED, on the fixed solver (40 000 iterations):
//   T_b = 293.251 K | T_w = 317.497 K | dT = 24.2454 K | U_b = 5.39407 m/s
//   Re = 28,768 | Nu = 64.4894 (was 64.5257: -0.06%)
//   y+ 56.88/57.77/58.57 | contErr 2.90e-8 -> 1.99e-8
//   thermostat power -3.20056 W against 3.2 W of wall heat (+0.106% ->
//     +0.0174%)
//   f MEASURED = 0.017140 (rho u_tau^2); the viscous form on the same faces
//     is tau_w 0.0866027 Pa, i.e. f = 0.019782
//   kinematic force balance -0.005% (unchanged)
//   40 000 iterations in 168 s, against 170 s before
//
// VERDICTS UNCHANGED: absolute prediction -5.9% (Gnielinski at the pipe
// f = 0.0238816 -> 68.5672), Dittus-Boelter -12.9%, Reynolds analogy +34.3%
// (still OPEN, still a FRICTION finding and not a thermal one). At PrtModel
// KaysCrawford: Nu 63.5527, -7.3% / -14.1% / +34.0%.
//
// See docs/07-lowmach-solver.md S1.1's last subsection and SPEC-LIT S26.1.
// ============================================================================
{
  "$schema": "https://meteor-cfd.msimul.com/schema/case-1.json",
  "name": "channelPeriodicFluxWF",

  "mesh": {
    "kind": "cartesian",
    "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "max": [0.08, 0.04, 0.04] },
    "cells":  [8, 6, 1],

    "boundaries": {
      "xmin": "streamwiseMin", "xmax": "streamwiseMax",
      "ymin": "wallHot0", "ymax": "wallHot1",
      "zmin": "back", "zmax": "front",
    },

    "cyclic": [ { "a": "streamwiseMin", "b": "streamwiseMax", "transform": "translate" } ],
  },

  "physics": {
    "gravity": [0, 0, 0],
    "fluid": { "nu": 1.5e-5, "Pr": 0.71, "Prt": 0.85, "TRef": 293.15 },
    "buoyancy": "densityRatio",
  },

  "turbulence": {
    "kind": "RAS",
    "model": "kEpsilon",
    "wallFunctions": { "kappa": 0.41, "E": 9.8 },
    "wallTreatment": "standard",
  },

  "sources": [
    { "type": "momentumSource", "field": "U", "bodyForce": [3.9, 0, 0] },
    // SPEC-LIT S35.1: replaces the fixed -heaterPower -3.2 compensation
    // (the closed-form -2 q_w A_wall this file's own header derives) with a
    // controller that measures the SAME balance instead of assuming it -
    // see this file's own header for why a fixed sink alone cannot pin
    // T's free additive constant. `target` is 293.15 K, this case's own
    // `initial.T`/`TRef`, so the WF leg's own already-recorded Nu (S1.1's
    // table) is reproduced rather than moved; `tau` 0.02 s is close to this
    // domain's own flow-through time (V^(1/3)/U_b ~ 0.017 s at the initial
    // U_b = 3 m/s). IDENTICAL target on both legs (see
    // `channelPeriodicFluxLowRe.jsonc`'s own copy) - the two legs are now
    // compared at the SAME bulk state, which S32/S34's fixed-sink versions
    // were not (S35's own point).
    { "type": "thermostat", "target": 293.15, "tau": 0.02, "weighting": "massFlux" },
  ],

  "patches": [
    {
      // SPEC-LIT S34.1: the 2-D constraint, not a boundary condition - no
      // per-field BC may be named alongside it (checked and refused).
      "match": "(back|front)",
      "kind": "empty",
    },
    {
      // The hot walls - SPEC-LIT S32.2's fixed-flux condition, named
      // explicitly with q_w. See this file's own header for how 500 W/m2
      // was chosen, and the `sources[]` thermostat above for what now
      // balances it (SPEC-LIT S35.1).
      "match": "wallHot.*",
      "kind": "wall",
      "U": { "type": "fixedValue", "value": [0, 0, 0] },
      "p": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
      "T": { "type": "fixedFluxTemperature", "q": 500.0 },
    },
    {
      "match": ".*",
      "kind": "wall",
      "U": { "type": "fixedValue", "value": [0, 0, 0] },
      "p": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
      "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" },
    },
  ],

  "initial": {
    "U": [3.0, 0, 0],
    "T": 293.15,
    "p": 0.0,
    "k": 0.03,
    "epsilon": 0.3,
    "nut": 0,
  },

  "numerics": {
    "algorithm": { "kind": "SIMPLE" },
    "ddt": "steadyState",
    "div": {
      "default": "Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,U)": "Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)",
      "div(phi,T)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,k)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
      "div(phi,epsilon)": "bounded Gauss upwind",
    },
    "grad": "Gauss linear",
    "laplacian": { "snGrad": "corrected", "nonOrthogonalCorrectors": 0 },
    "relaxation": { "U": 0.5, "p": 0.3, "T": 0.5, "k": 0.5, "epsilon": 0.5 },
    "solvers": [
      { "match": "p",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "DIC",      "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 1000 },
      { "match": "U",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.1,  "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "T",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "k",       "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
      { "match": "epsilon", "solver": "PBiCGStab", "preconditioner": "diagonal", "tolerance": 1e-9, "relTol": 0.01, "maxIter": 200 },
    ],
  },

  // Unused by `ofgpu-lowmach` in steady mode (no `-endTime`/`-deltaT` given on
  // the command line) - present because the schema requires it.
  "run": {
    "endTime": 1.0,
    "deltaT": 1.0,
    "adjustTimeStep": false,
  },
}
